# Web3 Audit Workflow — DeFi/Blockchain Attack Surface

## Attacker Philosophy
Web3 = code IS the law. If the contract has a bug, attacker wins. No WAF, no rate limit, no rollback.

## Workflow

### Phase 1: Contract Discovery
```bash
# Find contract addresses from website
curl -s "https://target.com" | grep -oP '0x[a-fA-F0-9]{40}' | sort -u

# Check if verified on Blockscout
curl -s "https://eth.blockscout.com/api/v2/addresses/{ADDR}" | python3 -c "
import sys,json
d=json.load(sys.stdin)
print(f'Contract: {d.get(\"name\",\"?\")}')
print(f'Verified: {d.get(\"is_verified\",\"?\")}')
print(f'Balance: {d.get(\"coin_balance\",\"?\")}')
"

# Get source code
curl -s "https://eth.blockscout.com/api/v2/addresses/{ADDR}/source-code"
```

### Phase 2: Static Analysis
```bash
# Slither (comprehensive)
slither . --print human-summary
slither . --detect reentrancy-eth,reentrancy-no-eth,reentrancy-unlimited-gas

# Aderyn (Solidity-specific)
aderyn .

# Mythril (symbolic execution)
myth analyze contracts/Contract.sol --execution-timeout 90
```

### Phase 3: Fuzzing
```bash
# Echidna (property-based)
echidna-test . --contract TestContract --test-limit 10000

# Medusa (alternative fuzzer)
medusa test

# Halmos (symbolic testing)
halmos --contract TestContract --function testSpecific
```

### Phase 4: Manual Review Checklist
```
□ Reentrancy (external calls before state updates)
□ Flash loan (price manipulation, governance attack)
□ Access control (unprotected functions, proxy hijack)
□ Oracle (single source, manipulable price)
□ Integer overflow (pre-0.8.0 or unchecked blocks)
□ Delegatecall (user-controlled target)
□ Bridge (message validation, validator compromise)
□ Governance (flashloan voting, snapshot exploit)
□ Fee-on-transfer (balance calculation errors)
□ Logic errors (math formulas, rounding)
```

### Phase 5: Fork Testing
```bash
# Fork mainnet and test
forge fork https://eth-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/KEY
forge test --match-test testExploit -vvvv
```

## Common Web3 Exploits
```
1. Flash loan → price manipulation → drain funds
2. Reentrancy → recursive withdrawal → drain funds
3. Access control → unprotected admin function → steal funds
4. Oracle manipulation → fake price → exploit lending
5. Signature replay → reuse old signature → drain funds
6. Proxy upgrade → hijack proxy → steal funds
```
